/* 
   d3doglregs.rky - shared registry key definitions between d3d and openGL.

   This file contains registry key definitions for keys that are used in either d3d9 or d3d10 and OGL. If you add, modify, or remove keys
   from this file, you should check with someone who is familiar with the module you AREN'T familiar with. (So OGL if you know D3D, for 
   example.) 

   Moving a key from one of the d3d registry description files, or creating one to match an existing key in opengl more or less constitutes
   creating an interface between these drivers. So it's important to keep in mind that changing the definition of a key here may affect
   another module, either breaking its build, or changing its behavior.

*/

/*
    REGISTRY KEY DESCRIPTION FILE

    This is your ONE STOP SHOP for adding new registry keys to the driver.
    If you add a reg key description in here, you automatically get several things:
    1) You get the several string needed to make the key work with basic functionality
    2) You can provide several values that the key can assume (or bitfields, etc) and
       those equates wil be generated for you
    3) D3DReg will get rebuilt and knows about the key and its possible values as well
       so you can instantly use the newly built d3dreg to start controlling your registry key.
    4) You can provide documentation for the registry key that is included with D3DReg
       and gets displayed as well. The documentation may be included in the driver
       as comments (although this isn't happening yet, but I'm adding it.)

    Here is the basic format for a registry key description. You must provide all
    the fields below which begin with YP_. All the other text are keywords which are
    understood by the parser.

BEGIN_KEY YP_keyname
    DefinedWhen:    YP_DefinedWhen
    RemappedName:   YP_RemappedName
    BinaryID:       YP_BinaryID
    Type:           YP_Type
    Protected
    Documentation:  YP_Documentation
    Values:         YP_ValueType:YP_ValueAttribute
    ImportFrom:     YP_keyname2 Values: YP_valname1 [, YP_valname2]*
    ImportFrom:     YP_keyname2 AllValues
       BEGIN_ENUM_LIST YP_EnumName*     (or BEGIN_DEFINE_LIST)
           YP_name1ForValue1, YP_name2ForValue1, ... YP_nameNForValue1 = YP_Value1, YP_Value1Documentation
           YP_name1ForValue2, YP_name2ForValue2, ... YP_nameNForValue2 = YP_Value2, YP_Value2Documentation
           .....
       END_ENUM_LIST                    (or END_DEFINE_LIST)
END_KEY


    YP_DefinedWhen

       This should be the argument to a preprocessor directive #if. It will surround all the equates generated by the parser for
       this registry key. You can use it, for example, to make sure that a debug only registry key is not referenced in the release build.
       e.g.  DefinedWhen:  defined(DEBUG) || defined(DEVELOP)
       If you want the key defined all the time just make YP_DefinedWhen a 1. e.g.  DefinedWhen:  1

    YP_RemappedName

       To make it a bit tougher for hackers and our competitors, the key names as stored in the registry are not the same as the
       visible names we use via D3DReg. This field is the remapped name that is stored in the registry. It is usually something
       like D3D_45874652. With this field, you must prefix with either a D3D_ or a D3DOGL_ and that depends upon whether or not
       this is a D3D only key or whether it is a key shared between D3D and OGL. To determine this you kinda just have to check
       to see how the control panel wants to deal with it. If the control panel is setting it for both D3D and OGL it will be the
       D3DOGL_ prefix; otherwise it will just be a D3D_ prefix. This prefix thing is kind of a legacy issue that heopfully will
       go away, but right now we need to keep it around. The numbers that follow are just random, but make sure that the remapped
       name you choose is not the same as any other remapped name in this file. e.g. RemappedName: D3D_86753098

    YP_BinaryID

       This is a 24 bit value that uniquely identifies a setting in this RKY file. The final 32 bit binary name of the setting is
       composed of YP_BinaryID of the lower 24 bits and the MODULE_PREFIX for the higher 8 bits [24..31]. This ensures that there 
       are no collisions across different module's setting names.

    Type

       This field gives the type of the registry key. Right now there are the following types:
       dword, qword, float, bool, binary, string. So the line would look like   Type: dword

    Protected
        This field is optional - if it's present, the registry key will not be zapped by the d3dreg command -zap.

    NvConfigureDriverSetting:  
        This field is optional - if it's present, the registry key will be used by the standalone tool 
        //sw/apps/gpu/drivers/common/configureDriver/... to expose selected DRS settings to the user.
        When modifying a key marked with NvConfigureDriverSetting, please check for dependencies in
        //sw/apps/gpu/drivers/common/configureDriver/inc/nvidia_settings.h.

    Documentation

       This is the primary documentation for the registry key. I encourage people to use it. This documentation will be
       displayed when a person uses the new d3dreg to dump out information about the registry key and its possible values.
       There are other palces in the registrry key description where you can provide documentation about each individual
       value the registry key can assume so this section is usually used for more general info about the key itself.
       However, you can put anything you want here. Everything in between quotes is taken to be the documentation.
       If you don't want to provide documentation, you still need to put two quotes with nothign between them.
       e.g. Documentation: "This is my documentation"       or      Documentation:  ""

    Values

        This section is OPTIONAL. In here you can provide values that the registry key uses. The equates are generated
        so that they can be used in the driver and d3dreg. If you don't need any special values or bitfields, then you
        don't need this section. There are MANY option here so follow along closely ;-)

       YP_ValueType

           This can be one  Samples, Bitfields or Range.

           Samples just means that you are going to list some values that the key can assume or that you want associated with the key.
               They have no special interpretation. In fact, the "values" may even be random strings. Samples is by far the most common YP_ValueType

           Bitfields means that EVERY value you list is a bit flag. These are treated specially by d3dreg because it is assumed that
               you can set more than one bitfield in a registry key whereas you canNOT do that with values that are marked as Samples.
               A Bitfield MUST be of Type: dword or you will get an error.

           Range means that the registry key is a value that needs to be in a certain range specified by a minimum and maximum. There
               can be several values in the enum list (described below), but one of them must be called MIN and one must be called MAX.
               d3dreg enforces this range when people enter a value for the key.

       YP_ValueAttribute

           This field is OPTIONAL and it has only one possible assignment right now -- LimitValuesToList
           This means that the only values that can be assigned to the registry key are the ones provided in the enum list (described below).
           In general, you probably want to set this. However, there are certainly cases where the registry key may have a large number of values
           and there are a few special values that you want to list but the rest of the possible values the user can just enter as a number.
           If you do NOT use this field, do not include the prior colon. e.g.    Values:  Samples

       ImportFrom: YP_keyname2 Values: YP_valname1 [, YP_valname2]*
       ImportFrom: YP_keyname2 AllValues
      
           This field is OPTIONAL and allows to import values with names YP_valname1 to YP_valnameN from another entry names YP_keyname2. The
           other entry must have been previously defined in the RKY file and must have the same type.
           Specifying Values: allows for a subset of the values form YP_keyname2 to be imported, AllValues imports all values. 
           Each value imported brings its name, numeric value (or string value) and description. It is possible to override later on the description.
           Imported values behave exactly as regular values.
           In the C header files there is no mention of the import, each imported value is defined again in the context of this entry. 

       BEGIN_ENUM_LIST/END_ENUM_LIST

           The parser generates the values as parts of a c++ enum, or as #defines in C header files.

       YP_EnumName

           This is the name given to your enum.
           Note: YP_EnumName MAY be going away soon. If you omit this name, one will be autogenerated based on the name of the key.

    YP_name1ForValue1, YP_name2ForValue1, ...
    YP_name1ForValue2, YP_name2ForValue2, ...

           Each value assignment in the values section can have multiple names associated with it.
           For example, you might have a Type: bool that has just the two possible boolean values, but they each have several names
           like this  ON, TRUE, 1, ENABLED  and   OFF, FALSE, 0, DISABLED
           The first name in each list of values will be stuffed in an enum or #define so that the driver can reference the value.
           All of the names in each list are provided to d3dreg, so that the user of d3dreg can type in any of the value names to get the desired result.

    YP_Value1, YP_Value2

           These are the values that are associated with the name lists above. These "values" may be strings or numbers (depending on the Type: XXXX field)

    YP_Value1Documentation, YP_Value2Documentation

           These are documentation for the individual values. I recommend using it as the documentation is dumped out in d3dreg and helps other people
           know what the value is supposed to do. The documentation should be inside quotation marks. If you do NOT have any documentation for a particular
           value, you must still put two qutoations marks with nothing inside them as a placeholder for the parser.

           NOTE!! You can add any preprocessor directives that you want around lines in the values section only!!
           That way you can add values which depend upon some other build option.

    Examples a registry key description

BEGIN_KEY  PS_SPORT
    DefinedWhen:    defined(RELEASE)
    RemappedName:   D3D_12345678
    BinaryID:       0xffbeef
    Type:           dword
    Documentation:  "Controls which sports are allowed"
    Values: Bitfields
       BEGIN_ENUM_LIST MySports
            RUN,  JOG,   SPRINT    = 0x00000001, "All forms of running"
            BIKE, CYCLE, RIDE      = 0x00000002, "All forms of cycling"
#if defined(DEBUG) || defined(ZMIRROR)
            FREE, BACK,  BUTTERFLY = 0x00000020, "All forms of swimming"
#endif
       END_ENUM_LIST
END_KEY

BEGIN_KEY  PS_MAGICIAN
    DefinedWhen:    1
    RemappedName:   D3DOGL_55667788
    BinaryID:       0xafbeef
    Type:           string
    Documentation:  "Controls which magician we want to use."
    Values: Samples:LimitValuesToList
       BEGIN_DEFINE_LIST
            TOPMAGICIAN, KINGARTHURSWIZARD, = "MERLIN",    ""
            COMICWIZARD, BLACKMAGICMAN,     = "DRSTRANGE", ""
       END_DEFINE_LIST
END_KEY


    USING REGISTRY KEYS AND THEIR EQUATES!!

    This file is parsed and a file called drivers\common\inc\drvregkeys.h is produced as part of the build. You can always check this if you
    are uncertain what the registry key names and values turned out to be after the parser produced it. However, here is the way it works.

    YP_keyname is used to produce two defines formed by adding an "_STRING" and an "_STRING_PLAIN" to the end of the name.
    Also a prefix is generated using the remapped name. Using the above examples, we have:

#define    PS_SPORT_STRING_PREFIX   "D3D_"
#define    PS_SPORT_STRING          "12345678"
#define    PS_SPORT_STRING_PLAIN    "PS_SPORT"

#define    PS_MAGICIAN_STRING_PREFIX   "D3DOGL_"
#define    PS_MAGICIAN_STRING          "55667788"
#define    PS_MAGICIAN_STRING_PLAIN    "PS_MAGICIAN"

    The enums/define values are all put in a different namespace (because many of the enum names may be the same amongst different registry keys).
    They are generated as follows:

namespace PS_SPORT_NAMESPACE
{
    enum PS_SPORT_ENUM
    {
        RUN  = 0x00000001,
        BIKE = 0x00000002,
#if defined(DEBUG) || defined(ZMIRROR)
        FREE = 0x00000020,
#endif
    };
}

namespace PS_MAGICIAN_NAMESPACE
{
    #define TOPMAGICIAN "MERLIN"
    #define COMICWIZARD "DRSTRANGE"
}

    Notice how only the FIRST name in the name list gets the define. d3dreg will understand all the values, but just for consistency in the driver,
    only the first one wil be included in the driver. So you should make the first name the "primary" one.

    NOTE!! It is possible that one of the values names may have begun with a number. Let's suppose I had called it 2BIKE instead of BIKE.
    Enum and define names are not allowed to begin with a number, so the name turns out to be _2BIKE instead. However, d3dreg will still
    use the name 2BIKE if you want to change the registry key value. This is really a minor point. In general, most of the names won't
    begin with a number.

    If you want to reference the values to use in your code, you have to either put this at the top of the file:

using namespace PS_SPORT_NAMESPACE;

    or, you can also prefix a given value name with the namespace like this:

    if (dwVariable & PS_SPORT_NAMESPACE::BIKE)
       do something;

    Note that choosing the "using namespace XXX" option might not always work. If yu have to add several "using namespace XXX" lines to the
    same file, you may find that there is some name collision. It isn't super likely, but it can happen, particulalry with registry booleans
    which will usually have a value name of ON and OFF (TRUE and FALSE) etc.. However the PS_XXX_NAMESPACE:: prefix can always be used in
    these circumstances to make it clear to the compiler which one you want.



    READING REGISTRY KEYS!

    There is new code to read registry keys. Look in drivers\ddraw\perfstrategies\regfuncs.cpp for the functions.
    drivers\ddraw\perfstrategies\nvPSBasicStats.cpp actually uses some of them if you want example code.
*/

RKY_VERSION 13
MODULE_STRING D3DOGL
      
BEGIN_KEY PS_VERTEX_SHADER_STATS_FLAGS
    DefinedWhen:        PERF_STRATEGY_REGKEYS
    BinaryID:           0xed52e4
    RemappedName:       D3D_487074847
    Type:               dword
    Documentation:      ""
    Values: Bitfields
        BEGIN_ENUM_LIST
            DUMP_NON_HAND_TUNED                         = 0x00000001, "Dumps non hand tuned vertex shaders"
            DUMP_HAND_TUNED                             = 0x00000002, "Dumps hand tuned vertex shaders"
            DUMP_VSFP                                   = 0x00000004, "Dumps the vertex shaders that are generated by vertex shader fixed pipe. Unlike the other dump types, the output shaders cannot be used as hand-tuned shaders due to the different input/output mapping. Therefore this dump is for informational purposes only. VSFP shaders are indicated in the dump by the labels BEGIN/END VSFP SHADER."
            DUMP_DECL                                   = 0x00000008, "Dumps all the DECLS that were created."
            DUMP_SYSTEM                                 = 0x00000010, "Dumps information for passthrough vertex shaders."
            DUMP_NVINST                                 = 0x00000020, "Dumps nvInst & COP args for the shader"
            DUMP_SOURCE_TOKENS                          = 0x00000080, "Dumps the D3D code token DWORDs."
            DUMP_ZERO_USAGE_SHADERS                     = 0x00000100, "By default, vertex shaders that were created by the app but not used are not dumped by the flags above. If you set this key, zero usage shaders are dumped."
            DUMP_SHADERS_AT_CREATE                      = 0x00001000, "Dumps the vertex shaders at the time of their creation, when a new GPU program is added. It overrides DUMP_ZERO_USAGE_SHADERS, as at creation time, there is no information about their usage. It can be useful for applications that do not explicitly destroy the allocated shaders, and consequently would not dump their content at deletion."
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_TIME                    = 0x00010000, "Measure Vertex shader execution time"
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_SPEED                   = 0x00020000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_STALLS                  = 0x00040000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_PCSAMPLER               = 0x00080000, ""
        END_ENUM_LIST
END_KEY

BEGIN_KEY PS_PIXEL_SHADER_STATS_FLAGS
    DefinedWhen:        PERF_STRATEGY_REGKEYS
    BinaryID:           0xe1e86b
    RemappedName:       D3D_27815290
    Type:               dword
    Documentation:      ""
    Values: Bitfields
        BEGIN_ENUM_LIST
            DUMP_NON_HAND_TUNED_TSS                                                       = 0x00000001, ""
            DUMP_HAND_TUNED_TSS                                                           = 0x00000002, ""
            DUMP_NON_HAND_TUNED_REAL                                                      = 0x00000004, ""
            DUMP_HAND_TUNED_REAL                                                          = 0x00000008, ""
            DUMP_NVINST                                                                   = 0x00000100, ""
            DUMP_CONST_HISTOGRAMS                                                         = 0x00001000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_TIME                                                      = 0x00010000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_SPEED                                                     = 0x00020000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_STALLS                                                    = 0x00040000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_MEASURE_PCSAMPLER                                                 = 0x00080000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_COMPILE_TIME_RUNTIME                                              = 0x00100000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_COMPILE_TIME                                                      = 0x00200000, ""
            DUMP_RAW_COMBINERS                                                            = 0x01000000, ""
            DUMP_TEXTURE_STAGE_INFO                                                       = 0x02000000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_API_BYTECODE                                                      = 0x04000000, ""
            DUMP_SHADER_USAGE_MAP                                                         = 0x08000000, ""
            DUMP_SURFACE_AND_STATE_INFO                                                   = 0x10000000, ""
            DUMP_ZERO_USAGE_SHADERS                                                       = 0x20000000, ""
            DUMP_FLAGS                                                                    = 0x40000000, ""
            DUMP_DSR_SHADERS                                                              = 0x80000000, ""
        END_ENUM_LIST
    Default: 0        
END_KEY

